30	    /// Promotes the use of `import type` for types.
31	    ///
32	    /// _TypeScript_ allows specifying a `type` keyword on an `import` to indicate that the `import` doesn't exist at runtime.
33	    /// This allows compilers to safely drop imports of types without looking for their definition.
34	    /// This also ensures that some modules are not loaded at runtime.
35	    ///
36	    /// The rule ensures that all imports used only as a type use a type-only `import`.
37	    /// It also groups inline type imports into a grouped `import type`.
38	    ///
39	    /// If you use the TypeScript Compiler (TSC) to compile your code into JavaScript,
40	    /// then you can disable this rule, as TSC can remove imports only used as types.
41	    /// However, for consistency and compatibility with other compilers, you may want to enable this rule.
42	    /// In that case we recommend to enable TSC's [`verbatimModuleSyntax`](https://www.typescriptlang.org/tsconfig/#verbatimModuleSyntax).
43	    /// This configuration ensures that TSC preserves imports not marked with the `type` keyword.
44	    ///
45	    /// You may also want to enable the editor setting [`typescript.preferences.preferTypeOnlyAutoImports`](https://devblogs.microsoft.com/typescript/announcing-typescript-5-3-rc/#settings-to-prefer-type-auto-imports) from the TypeScript LSP.
46	    /// This setting is available in Visual Studio Code.
47	    /// It ensures the `type` is used when the editor automatically imports a type.
48	    ///
49	    /// ## Caveat with TypeScript experimental decorators
50	    ///
51	    /// Some frameworks like Angular and NestJS rely on
52	    /// [experimental TypeScript decorators](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/decorators.html)
53	    /// which allow code to be generated based on type annotations.
54	    /// This is mainly used for dependency injection.
55	    ///
56	    /// Since Biome doesn't know how a decorator is implemented,
57	    /// it is unable to detect that an import used as a type is also used as a value in the code generated by a decorator.
58	    /// This leads Biome to suggest importing some imports as type, which are actually used as value at runtime.
59	    ///
60	    /// We haven't found a way to support this pattern yet.
61	    /// We recommend disabling this rule when using such decorators.
62	    ///
63	    /// ## Examples
64	    ///
65	    /// ### Invalid
66	    ///
67	    /// ```ts,expect_diagnostic
68	    /// import { A } from "./mod.js";
69	    /// type TypeOfA = typeof A;
70	    /// let a: A;
71	    /// ```
72	    ///
73	    /// ```ts,expect_diagnostic
74	    /// import { type A, type B } from "./mod.js";
75	    /// ```
76	    ///
77	    /// ```ts,expect_diagnostic
78	    /// import { type A, B } from "./mod.js";
79	    /// let c: A;
80	    /// let d: typeof B;
81	    /// ```
82	    ///
83	    /// ### Valid
84	    ///
85	    /// ```ts
86	    /// import type { A } from "./mod.js";
87	    /// let a: A;
88	    /// ```
89	    ///
90	    /// ```ts
91	    /// import { B } from "./mod.js";
92	    /// let a: B = new B();
93	    /// ```
94	    ///
95	    /// ```ts
96	    /// import { type A, B } from "./mod.js";
97	    /// let c: A;
98	    /// let d = new B();
99	    /// ```
100	    ///
101	    /// The rule ignores unused imports and imports with import attributes.
102	    ///
103	    /// ```ts
104	    /// import { A } from "./mod.js";
105	    ///
106	    /// import { B } from "./mod.js" with {};
107	    /// export type { B };
108	    /// ```
109	    ///
110	    /// ## Options
111	    ///
112	    /// This rule respects the [`jsxRuntime`](https://biomejs.dev/reference/configuration/#javascriptjsxruntime)
113	    /// setting and will make an exception for React globals if it is set to
114	    /// `"reactClassic"`.
115	    ///
116	    /// ### `style`
117	    ///
118	    /// The `style` option allows enforcing a style for importing types.
119	    /// The option supports three values:
120	    ///
121	    /// - `inlineType`: always use `import { type T }` instead of `import type { T }`
122	    /// - `separatedType`: always use `import type { T }` instead of `import { type T }`
123	    /// - `auto`: use both `import type { T }` and `import { type T }` (default)
124	    ///
125	    /// ```jsonc,options
126	    /// {
127	    ///     "options": {
128	    ///         "style": "inlineType"
129	    ///     }
130	    /// }
131	    /// ```
132	    ///
133	    /// ```ts,expect_diagnostic,use_options
134	    /// import { A } from "./mod.ts";
135	    /// export type { A };
136	    /// ```
137	    ///
138	    /// ```jsonc,options
139	    /// {
140	    ///     "options": {
141	    ///         "style": "separatedType"
142	    ///     }
143	    /// }
144	    /// ```
145	    ///
146	    /// ```ts,expect_diagnostic,use_options
147	    /// import { A } from "./mod.ts";
148	    /// export type { A };
149	    /// ```
150	    ///
151	    /// ```ts,expect_diagnostic,use_options
152	    /// import { type A, type B } from "./mod.ts";
153	    /// export type { A, B };
154	    /// ```
